From: Magnus Reimann <magnus.reimann@mailbox.org>
Subject: [PATCH glibc] hurd: fork: fetch the signal thread state before resuming it

_Fork stops the other threads with proc_dostop, copies the address
space, and resumes them with resume_threads before setting up the
child's threads.  Setting up the child's signal thread however calls
thread_get_state on our own signal thread, twice (for the machine state
and, on x86_64, in _hurd_tls_fork for the FS base).  thread_get_state
holds the target and waits for it to stop.

If the signal thread is meanwhile delivering a signal to the forking
thread, e.g. SIGCHLD from an earlier child, post_signal first suspends
that thread, and only then sees it is in a critical section and marks
the signal pending.  The forking thread is suspended while waiting in
thread_get_state, the signal thread is then stopped by the hold of
thread_get_state on its way back to user space, and each waits for the
other forever.

This was seen with gnumach running all processors in the default
processor set, as a sporadic hang of /bin/sh doing a command
substitution during boot:

  thread 1: __thread_get_state <- _Fork (_Fork.c:480) <- forkshell
  thread 2: __thread_suspend <- post_signal (hurdsig.c:1013, SIGCHLD)
            <- _S_msg_sig_post

with the forking thread suspended by the signal thread (user stop
count 1) and the signal thread held by thread_get_state (suspend count
1, user stop count 0).  It can happen on a uniprocessor too, but the
window is much smaller there.

Resume the other threads only once the state of the signal thread has
been fetched; it is still stopped by proc_dostop at that point.

---
--- a/sysdeps/mach/hurd/_Fork.c
+++ b/sysdeps/mach/hurd/_Fork.c
@@ -421,10 +421,6 @@
       for (i = 0; i < _hurd_nports; ++i)
 	__spin_unlock (&_hurd_ports[i].lock);
       ports_locked = 0;
-
-      /* All state has now been copied from the parent.  It is safe to
-	 resume other parent threads.  */
-      resume_threads ();
 
       /* Create the child main user thread and signal thread.  */
       if ((err = __thread_create (newtask, &thread))
@@ -510,6 +506,14 @@
 	LOSE;
       /* We do not thread_resume SIGTHREAD here because the child
 	 fork needs to do more setup before it can take signals.  */
+
+      /* All state has now been copied from the parent.  It is safe to
+	 resume other parent threads.  We only do so now because we have
+	 just fetched the state of our signal thread: had it been running,
+	 it could have been suspending this very thread to deliver a
+	 signal while thread_get_state waits for it to stop, and both
+	 threads would wait for each other forever.  */
+      resume_threads ();
 
       /* Set the child user thread up to return 1 from the setjmp above.  */
       _hurd_longjmp_thread_state (&state, env, 1);
